* arith_mixed.sno
* Benchmark: MIXED int/real arithmetic — the only shape that reaches rt_num_arith.
*
* WHY THIS FILE EXISTS (RTX-0d, s204).  arith_loop.sno calls rt_num_arith ZERO times at
* every loop count: SCRIP's emitter inlines integer arithmetic, so an integer-only program
* never reaches the C/asm runtime for it, and no amount of loop scaling changes that — the
* gap is structural, not a matter of window size.  Any ARITH A/B gated on an integer-only
* program is a guaranteed false null.  A real operand forces the runtime call.
*
* SHAPE: one real accumulator, one integer loop counter, no pattern matching, no I/O in the
* timed region.  Exactly ONE scalable literal (the LT bound, >= 1000), so the harness's
* auto-ranger adds PASSES and cannot alter the arithmetic being measured.
*
* DETERMINISTIC CHECKSUM, PREDICTED IN ADVANCE (not read off a run):
*   The loop body runs once more than the bound, because X is incremented BEFORE the LT test
*   that ends the loop.  So with bound P the accumulator ends at 1.5 * (P + 1), and CONVERT
*   to INTEGER truncates:      result = floor(1.5 * (P + 1))
*   P = 40000000  =>  60000001      <- the shipped bound
*   P = 80000000  => 120000001      <- scaling check
*   1.5 is exactly representable and 3(P+1)/2 stays far inside 2^53, so X is EXACT — this
*   checksum is not sensitive to floating-point rounding at any pass count the ranger picks.
    &TRIM = 1
    &STLIMIT = 999999999
    T1 = TIME()
    N = 0
    X = 0.0
LOOP    X = X + 1.5
    N = LT(N, 40000000) N + 1                 :S(LOOP)
    T2 = TIME()
    C = CONVERT(X, "INTEGER")
    OUTPUT = "result: " C
    OUTPUT = "ms: " (T2 - T1)
END
